In commit "kernel: expose btck_LogEntry" (94cf10c4bf8417e4543097c1e8673ad35485d1fd)

`Timestamp()` returns a `sys_time` but `MockTime()` returns `std::chrono::seconds`, which a duration, even though mock time is a point in time and 0 means no mock time is set. Since `LogEntry` is new, it would seem more correct to return `std::optional<std::chrono::sys_seconds>` which is a time point not a duration, with `std::nullopt` when `mocktime` is unset.

```c++
    //! Mock time in effect when the entry was logged, or std::nullopt if none was set.
    std::optional<std::chrono::sys_seconds> MockTime() const
    {
        if (m_entry->mocktime == 0) return std::nullopt;
        return std::chrono::sys_seconds{std::chrono::seconds{m_entry->mocktime}};
    }
```

In commit "kernel: expose btck_LogEntry" (94cf10c4bf8417e4543097c1e8673ad35485d1fd)

Minor: since `timestamp_ns` is the only time value in the kernel API in nanoseconds (the `mocktime` field below it, `btck_set_mock_time`, and the `btck_NotifyHeaderTip` timestamp are all seconds), and its actual precision depends on the platform's system clock, it might help to say both in the comment:

```c
    //! Wall-clock time the entry was logged, in nanoseconds since the Unix
    //! epoch. Unlike other time values in the kernel API, including the mocktime
    //! field below, which are in seconds, this is in nanoseconds. The actual
    //! precision depends on the platform's system clock and may be coarser
    //! (e.g. microseconds), in which case the low digits are zero.
    int64_t timestamp_ns;
```

